WEBVTT

1
00:00:15.124 --> 00:00:20.046
When I called on my friend Sherlock Holmes after Christmas to wish him happy holidays, he was lounging

2
00:00:20.106 --> 00:00:21.866
on his sofa in a purple bathrobe.

3
00:00:22.626 --> 00:00:28.308
On his right were a pipe rack and a pile of crumpled morning papers, evidently just read.

4
00:00:28.348 --> 00:00:34.530
On his left, hanging on a wooden chair, was a very seedy, disreputable-looking felt hat that was worn

5
00:00:34.550 --> 00:00:36.010
and cracked in several places.

6
00:00:36.931 --> 00:00:39.651
Holmes was holding a magnifying glass and medical forceps.

7
00:00:40.272 --> 00:00:44.853
He pinched the hat with the forceps and lifted it to examine it under the lens.

8
00:00:44.873 --> 00:00:46.674
"You're busy," I said.

9
00:00:46.694 --> 00:00:47.634
"I'm interrupting you."

10
00:00:48.394 --> 00:00:49.895
"Not at all," Holmes said.

11
00:00:49.915 --> 00:00:51.215
"I'm glad you're here.

12
00:00:51.775 --> 00:00:54.376
The matter at hand is trivial but still interesting."

13
00:00:55.152 --> 00:00:58.696
I sat in his armchair and warmed my hands before his crackling fire.

14
00:00:59.377 --> 00:01:03.201
Frosty conditions had set in, and the windows were thick with ice crystals.

15
00:01:03.221 --> 00:01:09.849
"I suppose," I said, "that as ordinary as that hat looks, there's some deadly story to it, or

16
00:01:09.889 --> 00:01:11.971
it's the clue to some mystery or crime."

17
00:01:12.832 --> 00:01:13.833
"No, no.

18
00:01:13.974 --> 00:01:15.856
No crime," Holmes said.

19
00:01:16.772 --> 00:01:22.935
"Only one of those whimsical little incidents that happen when you have four million human beings jostling each

20
00:01:22.995 --> 00:01:25.236
other within the space of a few square miles.

21
00:01:25.936 --> 00:01:27.897
Here in London anything's possible.

22
00:01:28.517 --> 00:01:32.939
Many problems can be peculiar, however, without being criminal, as we've seen before."

23
00:01:32.959 --> 00:01:35.020
"That's true," I agreed.

24
00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:38.181
"Half of your last six cases involved no crime."

25
00:01:38.201 --> 00:01:40.302
"Precisely," said Holmes.

26
00:01:40.322 --> 00:01:44.744
"And I think this small matter should fall into the same innocent category.

27
00:01:45.604 --> 00:01:47.366
You know Peterson, the security guard?"

28
00:01:47.386 --> 00:01:50.149
"Yes." "This evidence belongs to him."

29
00:01:50.169 --> 00:01:51.430
"It's his hat?"

30
00:01:51.910 --> 00:01:52.231
I said.

31
00:01:52.251 --> 00:01:54.913
"No. No, he found it.

32
00:01:55.454 --> 00:01:56.695
Its owner is unknown.

33
00:01:57.516 --> 00:02:01.379
But don't see it as a battered hat; see it as an intellectual problem.

34
00:02:02.280 --> 00:02:04.262
First how did it get here?

35
00:02:04.782 --> 00:02:10.067
It arrived on Christmas morning, along with a fat goose, which is probably roasting in Peterson's oven right

36
00:02:10.127 --> 00:02:16.257
now. Peterson, who we know is a very honest fellow, shared what he knows about the situation with

37
00:02:16.317 --> 00:02:17.858
me." I nodded.

38
00:02:17.878 --> 00:02:23.719
"At about four o'clock in the morning, Peterson was walking home from work, still in his uniform, down

39
00:02:23.800 --> 00:02:24.940
Tottenham Court Road.

40
00:02:25.840 --> 00:02:30.841
By the light of a street lamp, he saw a tall man ahead, walking with a slight stagger

41
00:02:31.262 --> 00:02:34.142
and carrying a white goose slung over his shoulder.

42
00:02:34.923 --> 00:02:39.784
As Peterson watched, a gang of young men stopped this stranger and an argument broke out.

43
00:02:40.536 --> 00:02:42.237
One of them knocked the man's hat off."

44
00:02:43.137 --> 00:02:43.677
"Anyone hurt?"

45
00:02:44.097 --> 00:02:44.437
I asked.

46
00:02:45.237 --> 00:02:45.998
Holmes shook his head.

47
00:02:46.018 --> 00:02:48.698
"The man raised his walking stick to defend himself.

48
00:02:49.179 --> 00:02:54.120
However, he accidentally smashed the shop window behind him as he swung the stick.

49
00:02:55.160 --> 00:03:00.702
Peterson rushed to help, but the man, shocked at having broken the window and seeing someone in uniform

50
00:03:00.762 --> 00:03:02.883
running toward him, dropped his goose.

51
00:03:04.163 --> 00:03:07.544
He ran off and vanished in the maze of small streets.

52
00:03:08.572 --> 00:03:13.854
The gang also fled when they saw Peterson, and he was left with this battered hat and a

53
00:03:13.894 --> 00:03:15.615
delicious Christmas goose."

54
00:03:16.776 --> 00:03:18.976
"Peterson surely returned the items," I said.

55
00:03:18.997 --> 00:03:21.237
"My dear fellow, that's the problem.

56
00:03:21.257 --> 00:03:22.838
'For Mrs.

57
00:03:22.938 --> 00:03:29.661
Henry Baker' was printed on a small card tied to the bird's left leg, and the initials 'H.B.'

58
00:03:29.821 --> 00:03:31.682
are visible on the lining of the hat.

59
00:03:32.722 --> 00:03:38.125
But as there are hundreds of Henry Bakers in this city of ours, it's not easy to restore

60
00:03:38.185 --> 00:03:39.606
lost property to any of them."

61
00:03:40.486 --> 00:03:41.487
"What did Peterson do?"

62
00:03:41.847 --> 00:03:42.127
I said.

63
00:03:42.147 --> 00:03:47.650
"He brought me the hat and goose, knowing that even the smallest problems interest me.

64
00:03:48.471 --> 00:03:53.854
Not long after, Peterson and I agreed that the bird might spoil and should be eaten immediately.

65
00:03:54.714 --> 00:04:00.477
So he carried it off, while I kept the hat of the unknown gentleman who lost his Christmas

66
00:04:00.517 --> 00:04:04.319
dinner." "How are you ever going to find out who owns the hat?"

67
00:04:04.640 --> 00:04:05.060
I asked.

68
00:04:05.952 --> 00:04:09.395
"We have everything we need right here," Holmes said.

69
00:04:09.415 --> 00:04:10.876
"From this old hat?"

70
00:04:11.197 --> 00:04:11.457
I said.

71
00:04:11.477 --> 00:04:14.359
"Precisely." "You're joking."

72
00:04:14.920 --> 00:04:15.340
I laughed.

73
00:04:16.201 --> 00:04:17.482
"Here's my magnifying glass.

74
00:04:17.922 --> 00:04:18.823
You know my methods.

75
00:04:19.424 --> 00:04:22.586
What can you tell me about the man who wore this hat?"

76
00:04:23.507 --> 00:04:25.329
I turned the tattered hat over gingerly.

77
00:04:26.029 --> 00:04:30.713
It was an ordinary black hat of the usual round shape, hard and well worn.

78
00:04:31.634 --> 00:04:33.916
The red silk lining was very discolored.

79
00:04:34.740 --> 00:04:39.241
There was no brand name, but as Holmes said, the initials "H.B."

80
00:04:39.301 --> 00:04:40.762
were scrawled on one side.

81
00:04:41.622 --> 00:04:48.484
The outer part of the hat was cracked, exceedingly dusty, and spotted in several places, although there appeared

82
00:04:48.524 --> 00:04:53.065
to be some attempt to hide the discolored patches by smearing them with ink.

83
00:04:53.085 --> 00:04:56.346
"I see nothing," I said, handing it back to my friend.

84
00:04:56.366 --> 00:04:59.827
"On the contrary, Watson, you see everything.

85
00:05:00.587 --> 00:05:03.688
You fail, however, to think logically about what you see."

86
00:05:04.400 --> 00:05:06.461
"Then tell me what you infer from this hat."

87
00:05:07.021 --> 00:05:13.324
He picked it up, gazed at it, and promptly became lost in thought—one of Holmes' peculiar habits.

88
00:05:14.444 --> 00:05:20.987
"It could suggest more," he said finally, "but a few conclusions are certain, and a few others are

89
00:05:21.047 --> 00:05:21.867
at least probable.

90
00:05:23.048 --> 00:05:29.630
It's obvious the man is highly intellectual and was financially well-off at least three years ago, but he

91
00:05:29.670 --> 00:05:31.591
has fallen upon hard times.

92
00:05:32.451 --> 00:05:33.392
He probably drinks.

93
00:05:34.120 --> 00:05:38.024
That may account for the obvious fact that his wife no longer loves him."

94
00:05:38.844 --> 00:05:39.985
"My dear Holmes!"

95
00:05:40.005 --> 00:05:43.849
"He still has some self-respect though," he continued, ignoring me.

96
00:05:43.869 --> 00:05:46.431
"He's middle-aged and has gray hair.

97
00:05:47.011 --> 00:05:51.115
Oh, his hair was cut in the last few days, and he uses hair cream.

98
00:05:51.816 --> 00:05:55.058
Also I doubt his house is lit by gas."

99
00:05:55.078 --> 00:05:57.140
"You're certainly joking, Holmes."

100
00:05:57.160 --> 00:05:58.321
"Not at all.

101
00:05:59.062 --> 00:06:01.164
Surely you can see how I got these results."

102
00:06:02.264 --> 00:06:05.185
"No doubt I am very stupid, but I don't.

103
00:06:05.866 --> 00:06:08.066
Why do you think this man is intellectual?"

104
00:06:09.007 --> 00:06:10.067
Holmes put the hat on.

105
00:06:10.507 --> 00:06:14.069
It fell down over his forehead and settled on the bridge of his nose.

106
00:06:14.089 --> 00:06:16.530
"Size," he said.

107
00:06:16.550 --> 00:06:19.831
"A man with so large a brain must have something in it."

108
00:06:19.851 --> 00:06:22.152
"And the decline of his fortunes?"

109
00:06:22.872 --> 00:06:28.595
"This style of hat, with its slightly curled-up brim, first appeared in shops three years ago.

110
00:06:29.555 --> 00:06:31.676
This hat is of the very best quality.

111
00:06:32.124 --> 00:06:34.905
See the band of ribbed silk and the excellent lining?

112
00:06:35.886 --> 00:06:40.068
This man bought this expensive hat three years ago but no hat since.

113
00:06:40.688 --> 00:06:42.449
He's gone down in the world."

114
00:06:42.469 --> 00:06:45.310
"Well, that's clear enough," I said.

115
00:06:45.330 --> 00:06:46.830
"What about his self-respect?"

116
00:06:47.771 --> 00:06:48.831
Sherlock Holmes laughed.

117
00:06:50.172 --> 00:06:53.313
"He tried to conceal some of the stains on the felt with ink.

118
00:06:54.034 --> 00:06:57.415
That's a sign he hasn't entirely lost his sense of pride."

119
00:06:57.435 --> 00:06:59.516
"That's plausible, I suppose."

120
00:07:00.140 --> 00:07:04.561
"His age and hair cream can be determined by close examination of the lower lining.

121
00:07:05.341 --> 00:07:11.163
The magnifying glass shows a large number of gray hair ends, clean cut by a barber's scissors.

122
00:07:11.963 --> 00:07:12.884
They appear sticky.

123
00:07:13.384 --> 00:07:14.084
Now sniff."

124
00:07:15.124 --> 00:07:16.504
I bent over the hat and inhaled.

125
00:07:16.524 --> 00:07:21.586
"Lime." "A popular ingredient in hair cream," said Holmes, nodding.

126
00:07:21.606 --> 00:07:24.347
"Now inspect the dust.

127
00:07:25.127 --> 00:07:27.547
It's not a grainy, gray street dust.

128
00:07:28.208 --> 00:07:30.393
It's fluffy brown: house dust.

129
00:07:30.773 --> 00:07:33.335
The hat is hung indoors most of the time."

130
00:07:33.355 --> 00:07:37.297
"But his wife—you said she's stopped loving him."

131
00:07:37.317 --> 00:07:40.640
"This hat hasn't been brushed clean for weeks.

132
00:07:41.380 --> 00:07:45.723
When I see you with a week's worth of dust on your hat, my dear Watson, I shall

133
00:07:45.803 --> 00:07:48.225
fear you've lost your wife's affection.

134
00:07:48.845 --> 00:07:50.967
No wife would allow her husband to go out like that."

135
00:07:51.627 --> 00:07:53.108
"He might be a bachelor," I said.

136
00:07:53.128 --> 00:07:56.310
"No, the goose was for his wife.

137
00:07:56.851 --> 00:07:58.472
Remember the card on the bird's leg?"

138
00:08:00.041 --> 00:08:01.262
"You have an answer for everything.

139
00:08:01.782 --> 00:08:04.104
How do you know his house is not lit by gas?"

140
00:08:05.525 --> 00:08:08.948
"One candle-grease stain, or even two, might be accidental.

141
00:08:09.889 --> 00:08:11.170
I see more than five.

142
00:08:11.830 --> 00:08:18.196
He comes in frequent contact with burning candles—walks upstairs at night probably, his hat in one hand, a

143
00:08:18.556 --> 00:08:19.837
dripping candle in the other.

144
00:08:20.798 --> 00:08:23.760
Anyway, he never got these stains from a gas jet.

145
00:08:24.181 --> 00:08:28.144
Satisfied?" "Very clever," I said, laughing.

146
00:08:29.124 --> 00:08:34.707
"But since no crime was committed and no harm done except for the loss of a goose, all

147
00:08:34.747 --> 00:08:36.268
this seems to be a waste of energy."

148
00:08:37.428 --> 00:08:41.190
As Sherlock Holmes opened his mouth to reply, his door flew open.

149
00:08:41.851 --> 00:08:44.332
Peterson rushed in, his cheeks bright red.

150
00:08:44.352 --> 00:08:46.033
"The goose, Mr.

151
00:08:46.073 --> 00:08:46.413
Holmes!"
